#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The overnight shift's command line.

Three parts live here.

`overnight intake --dry-run` (spec T3a) reads the board and the open pull
requests and turns them into intake records. It reads only: it writes no
record and keeps no list of its own. The ticket CLI refreshes its own
cache when it reads the board, and that cache is the ticket CLI's, not a
queue of the shift's.

`overnight filter --dry-run` (spec T3b) filters that board: it drops the
Ascend work, the holds, the tickets that need Shyam, the tickets that
need a design and the tickets with an open question in the coordinator's
decisions log, and it prints every drop with its reason. It reads only.
`overnight check-pr --repo <repo> --number <n>` is the second check: it
refuses any pull request in an Ascend repository. Both fail closed: a
read that fails is a refusal, never a pass.

    overnight intake --dry-run
    overnight filter --dry-run
    overnight check-pr --repo <repo> --number <n>

`overnight board --json` (spec T3d) returns the whole board for the
day tick: every board ticket and every open pull request the coordinator
may act on, in the order the shift works them, and the one action each
item needs now (start the ready work, queue a gate for an open head that
has none, send a failed gate's findings back to its builder). It reads
only: it writes no file and keeps no list. The board is the list.

    overnight board --json

`overnight report` (spec T11) renders the morning report from the shift's
own records: the receipts, the parking lot, the shift log and the policy
snapshot. It never keeps a second queue and it invents nothing. A count it
cannot check is written as UNVERIFIED, and a check is never reported as
passed unless the records say it ran.

    overnight report --dry-run

`overnight shift` (spec T12, revision 3) is the command the launchd job
fires. The job fires it on a fixed interval, every day: the shift runs
24x7, so there is no night window and no part of the night. Every run is
one cycle: it reads the policy snapshot, reads the whole board under the
snapshot's allow list, works it (there is no item cap and no allowance
stop) and writes a receipt for every item whose state changed. The shift
is never armed and never unarmed. The first cycle of the morning writes
the day's morning report, so Shyam reads one report in the morning;
`--report` writes it on demand.

    overnight shift
    overnight shift --report

`overnight receipt` (spec T5b) writes the receipt of every item whose state
the shift can see: the state, the pull request, the checks and their exit
codes, the gate's own verdict, the files the pull request touches, the
builder's own time and why the item stopped. Every field is read from
GitHub, the queue or a thread, so a receipt never carries a model claim
where a proof belongs. The receipt is written on every state change of an
item and is rewritten when the item's state or its evidence changes.

    overnight receipt --repo shyamsivakumar/mac-ops --number 181
    overnight receipt --items 3

`overnight close` (spec T6) closes a ticket's issue, and only when the
receipt holds the evidence: a command, its exit code and a gate PASS on
the pull request. The receipt is the proof, so a receipt that holds a
claim without an exit code leaves the issue open and the command says
which record is missing.

    overnight close --repo shyamsivakumar/mac-ops --number 182

`overnight policy` (spec T4, revision 3) writes the policy snapshot the
shift reads on every cycle: `run/policy.json`. The shift is never unarmed
and has no deadline, so the snapshot is the whole contract. It holds the
allow list, the queue's slot cap (read only, never raised), the stall cap,
the retry cap and the verify level. It has no item cap and no allowance
stop: the whole backlog is in scope and model use is never a stop
condition.

    overnight policy

`overnight redact` (spec T7a) is the one redaction the shift has. Every
record it writes under `.overnight/` goes through it, so no receipt, no
shift log line and no morning report holds a token, a key, a cookie or a
`.env` value. The command shows the redaction on a fixture: the fake key
goes in and does not come out.

    overnight redact --file fixture.txt
    overnight redact --text 'ghp_...'

The console is a writer as well (spec T7b). Launchd writes everything the
module prints to the job's own log, and a printed line can carry a secret:
a gate thread's report, a command's own output tail, a pull request title.
So every line the module prints goes through the same redaction function
(`emit`), and the job log holds no more than the records under
`.overnight/` do.

The clock is the Mac's local clock, the clock launchd fires on. The
report and the log say Pacific time.

The board is the list. Intake reads `ticket list --fresh --json` for the
tickets and `gh pr list` for each allowed repository, and holds the records
in memory for the run. The later parts of the spec (ordering, arming and
the tick) build on the same records.
"""

